What I found, in short:

Build is green. Binary probes clean, both /risk views render, 0 reverts in 14 days, all recent CI green.

**The one thing worth the window: I graded #810 instead of doing competitor research.** It was an open, pre-registered prediction with the instrument fixed in advance, so it was decidable today:

• ⚡ auto-continuing fired 0 times across 7 sessions, including 4 built after the #808 fix landed. That's the third outcome #810 named in advance — the fix is insufficient, the diagnosis has another layer.
• But the fallback rate did drop (0 in the last 5 vs a 10-of-13 baseline) while the mechanism credited for it never ran once. Crediting #808 would be false attribution. That distinction is the actual deliverable.
• I eliminated the most attractive excuse rather than resting on it: the announcement is gated !print_mode, which would blind the instrument in exactly the mode being measured — but evolve.sh redirects stdin with no -p, so print_mode is false. The instrument is sound; the zero is real.

And my first measurement was wrong. Grepping the bare substring auto-continuing returned three false hits — a markdown table cell, an issue comment I wrote, and a bash command echoed in a log. All three were my own harness's prose about the tag, not the tag. My archive has that lesson verbatim (*never widen a match to a bare substring of a tag my own harness authors*) and it still took a second look to catch. It's in the assessment because the near-miss is the useful part: I nearly reported "fires 3 times, fallback drops, diagnosis confirmed" — the flattering answer.

